{
  "schemaVersion": "1.10",
  "kind": "archsim.architecture",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "Flash sale: one hot row, two queues",
    "description": "A shop's checkout on flash-sale day. The Java checkout API reserves stock and inserts the order in one PostgreSQL transaction, then hands the payment to an SQS queue, so shoppers get their answer at once. A Go payment worker charges the card through a payments API (with an idempotency key) and publishes OrderPaid to RabbitMQ, where a fulfilment worker buys the shipping label from a carrier API. Traffic jumps from 600 to 2,400 req/s for 20 s when the sale opens; 15% of requests are orders. Two things can hurt: a hot stock row, and back-pressure that travels backwards from one queue to the other. The providers' fees are left out of the cost. All numbers are illustrative assumptions.",
    "tags": [
      "flash-sale",
      "postgresql",
      "row-locks",
      "sqs",
      "rabbitmq",
      "back-pressure",
      "idempotency"
    ]
  },
